That doesn't make sense for the linear regression.  The
beta are unobserved parameters that are fit by observing
x and y.

> The model becomes
>
> --------------------------------
> data {
> int<lower=0> N;
> vector[N] beta_1;
> vector[N] beta_2;
> vector[N] y;
> }
> parameters {
> real x_1;
> real x_2;
> real<lower=0> sigma;
> }
> model {
> y ~ normal(beta_1 * x_1 + beta_2 * x_2, sigma);
> }
> --------------------------------

That just turns around the names, making beta the data and x_1 the
parameters.

> #3 Now, let's suppose I don't want just the real x, but I want x_mean and x_sd. How do I do it?
>
> --------------------------------
> data {
> int<lower=0> N;
> vector[N] beta_1;
> vector[N] beta_2;
> vector[N] y;
> }
> parameters {
> real x_1_mean;
> real x_2_mean;
> real x_1_sd;
> real x_2_sd;
> real<lower=0> sigma;
> }
> model {
> y ~ normal(beta_1 * ??? + beta_2 * ???, sigma);
> }
> --------------------------------
>
> I hope this proof of principle is a bit clearer.

I'm afraid not.

I'd recommend thinking generatively in terms of how
everything comes about: parameters beta and sigma
from priors, predictors x from priors, and then y from
x, beta, and sigma.

Looked at that way, the density function f(beta, sigma, x, y)
never changes, just what you observe does.

Only the x and y are set up to be directly observable here,
which is why I'm confused about what you're trying to do.

And I don't see where means come into play at all.

- Bob

