messages.body_plain:
The problem is the following. You have a matrix V and matrices Q,W. All have dimension nxm. You want to select certain elements from V and use them to increase the log-likelihood. The elements are selected based on the corresponding values from the "condition matrices" Q and W.

The basic (non-vectorized) code looks like this

matrix[n,m] lik_mat;
lik_mat=rep_mat(0,n,m); //collects likelihood values

for (i in 1:n){
     for (j in 1:m){

if (Q[i,j]==1){  //condition 1

  if (W[i,j]>0) { //condition 2
  lik_mat[i,j]=gumbel_lpdf(log(V[i,j]])|0,sigmaeps);}

  if (W[i,j]==0){
  lik_mat[i,j]=gumbel_lcdf(log([V[i,j])|0,sigmaeps);}
}
}
}
target+=sum(lik_mat)

The problem is making the selection, taking the log from the selected values and then either applying gumbel_lpdf or gumbel_lcdf. I am not sure how to improve from the basic loop-case.

I would appreciate any ideas or tips you may have in making this faster.

Just allocate a dense N x 2 matrix and ignore
the entries you don't need.  You can encode
the indexes to ignore, or even create an N x 2
matrix of 1 and 0 values to indicate which ones
are included.

If the bivariate distribution is easily marginalized
into univariate distributions, like say a normal,
you'd use those on the observed components.  So
that'd be:

vector[2] y[N];
int<lower=0, upper=1> y_observed[N, 2];

for (n in 1:N) {
  if (y_observed[n, 1] && y_observed[n, 2])
    y[n] ~ multi_normal(mu, Sigma);
  else if (y_observed[n, 1])
    y[n, 1] ~ normal(mu[1], sqrt(Sigma[1, 1]));
  else if (y_observed[n, 2])
    y[n, 2] ~ normal(mu[2], sqrt(Sigma[2, 2]));
}

But...  that's not very efficient.  It's much better
to vectorize all of this.  So, in transformed data,
build up three vectors of indices, for the three cases
above:

transformed data {
  int ns12[observed_12(y_observed)];
  int ns1[observed_1(y_observed)];
  int ns2[observed_2(y_observed)];

you need to do this with functions to count because you need to declare
sizes.  Then the rest of transformed data just fills (I'm going to
use the syntax about to release in 2.13):

  int n12 = 1;
  int n1 = 1;
  int n2 = 1;
  for (n in 1:N) {
    if (y_observed[n, 1] && y_observed[n, 2]) {
      ns12[n12] = n;
      n12 = n12 + 1;
    } else if (y_observed[n, 1]) {
      ns1[n1] = n;
      n1 = n1 + 1;
    } else if (y_observed[n, 2]) {
      ns2[n2] = n;
      n2 = n2 + 1;
    }
  }

and then, in the model block, everything's nice and vectorizable
using those indexes constructed once in transformed data:

  y[ns12] ~ multi_normal(mu, Sigma);
  y[ns1] ~ normal(mu[1], sqrt(Sigma[1, 1]));
  y[ns2] ~ normal(mu[2], sqrt(Sigma[2, 2]));

This is much more efficient than using latent variables for the
missing data, but requires the multivariate distribution to be marginalized
analytically.

I'll add this discussion to the missing data chapter:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/2122#issuecomment-263031536

- Bob

> On Nov 25, 2016, at 3:56 AM, xi...@uci.edu wrote:
>
> Hi Bob,
>
> So I want to try both approaches in terms of declaring the missing data elements as parameters or ignoring them. When I ignore the missing data, I need to get rid of the corresponding the matrix entries of my ODE solution. This is easy in a row vector, when I can just eliminate elements and shrink the length of the ODE solution vector. However, for a bivariate matrix in which different elements and different numbers of elements are missing on each row, I can't just delete elements, since it is obviously impossible to have a matrix with different sized rows. What would be the protocol for ignoring elements in this multivariate case? This is a question I was trying to get at earlier, but could not mentally phrase.
