Great example.  But the way you coded the model, it assumes
that N can vary by trial, whereas you said you wanted it to
vary once.  So each mixture component has to encompass all
the data, which is nice, because it lets us vectorize that
binomial:

data {
  int K;
  int Y[K];
}
transformed data {
  int N[10];

  for (s in 1:10)

    N[s] = 10 + s;
}
parameters{
  real<lower=0, upper=1> theta;
}
transformed parameters {

  vector[10] lp;
  for (s in 1:10)
    lp[s] = log(0.1) + binomial_lpmf(Y | N[s], theta);
}
model {
  theta ~ beta(7,2);
  target += log_sum_exp(lp);
}
generated quantities {
  simplex[10] Pr_N_eq;
  Pr_N_eq = exp(lp - log_sum_exp(lp));
}

The generated quantities provide the probability estimate in the posterior
for 10 + s.  You might want to skip the external exp because it's
going to do a lot of rounding to zero in the tails.

Then you should have them plot the pairs the posterior log density
for theta and N---you can do that without any fancy code to see the
ridge running along theta * N = expected number of outcomes.

The program's not quite right, because it allows the possibility
that the number of successes given is greater than the possible
number if you go from 11 to 20.  For instance, if the true value is
15 and theta = 0.9, you're likely to get more than 11 successes, so
11 can't be the value of the parameter.  I stayed out of that region
by simulating a low-ish value of theta:

theta <- 0.40;
N <- 12;
K <- 200;
Y <- rep(N, 0);
for (k in 1:K)
  Y[k] = rbinom(1, N, theta);

library(rstan);
fit <- stan("lambert-coins.stan", data=list(K = K, Y = Y));
print(fit, probs=c(0.25, 0.5, 0.75), c("Pr_N_eq", "theta", "lp__"), digits=2)

